<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<Version>5.2.9-local.5</Version>
		<!-- Brief current-version highlights only. Full per-version history with code samples lives in CHANGELOG.md (linked from the README). -->
		<PackageReleaseNotes>v5.2.8 - TWO disposal fixes. (1) A disposed Context kept everything it ever built: IRContext.Dispose and IRTypeContext.Dispose disposed only their LOCKS, so every Method, the whole value graph hanging off it, and the entire interned type universe stayed referenced by the disposed object, and Accelerator disposal released no kernel cache. Harmless on desktop, where the Context is collected moments later; in a browser the Context is rooted, so that graph is pinned for the life of the page and is what exhausts the Wasm managed heap. Disposal now routes through ClearCache(Everything). MEASURED: steady-state growth per Context 0.73 MiB -> 0.20 MiB, a 3.6x reduction. Note this mitigates rather than removes the underlying browser-only rooting, which is still under investigation. (2) Context.Dispose now releases BROWSER device handles. WebGPU device enumeration calls RequestAdapter and the resulting GPUAdapter is held by the registered ILGPU Device for the life of the Context; nothing released it, so every Context.Create() abandoned one adapter and pinned real GPU driver resources for the life of the page. It went unnoticed because upstream Device is deliberately not IDisposable - a DESKTOP device is a description (a CUDA ordinal, an OpenCL device id) that owns nothing, while a BROWSER device owns a live JS handle. Dispose now releases any registered device that implements IDisposable; WebGPUILGPUDevice and WebGLILGPUDevice do, desktop devices do not and are unaffected. MEASURED against the live SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML demo via SpawnJS's interop slot table: +1 rooted GPUAdapter per test surviving a forced GC before, and a return to the startup baseline of 5 slots with zero adapters after. This is a large part of what the Wasm lane's "Garbage collector could not allocate 16384u bytes" heap exhaustion was made of, since a browser test run creates a Context per test. NEW Context.ContextDisposeTrace (default off) traces each step of Dispose and reports how many devices it released - turn it on before theorising about Context lifetime. Fork bundle moves to 2.3.2 because the change is in the fork assembly, not this wrapper.</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>True</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<EmbedAllSources>true</EmbedAllSources>
		<DebugType>Embedded</DebugType>
		<Title>SpawnDev.ILGPU</Title>
		<Authors>LostBeard</Authors>
		<Description>.Net GPU compute for the browser and desktop. Write once, run on WebGPU, WebGL, Wasm, CUDA, OpenCL, and CPU.</Description>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU</PackageProjectUrl>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE.txt</PackageLicenseFile>
		<PackageIcon>icon-128.png</PackageIcon>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU.git</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageTags>Blazor;BlazorWebAssembly;Cuda;OpenCL;WebGPU;WebGL;WebAssembly;Multithreading;WebWorkers;GPGPU;ILGPU;ParallelProcessing;ComputeShader;CrossPlatform</PackageTags>
		<NoPackageAnalysis>true</NoPackageAnalysis>
		<EnableDefaultCompileItems>true</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
		<IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
		<LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
		<AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.SpawnJS" Version="2.1.10" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The unit-test project asserts internal invariants (e.g. the cache-key drift guard). -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU.Demo.Shared" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- =====================================================================
	     CRITICAL: FOUR-PACKAGE BUNDLE - read before bumping any one of these.
	     =====================================================================
	     A bug fixed under ILGPU/ ships in ILGPU.dll which lives in the SEPARATE
	     SpawnDev.ILGPU.Fork nupkg, NOT in this SpawnDev.ILGPU wrapper. If you
	     bump only this csproj, consumers transitively pull the OLD Fork from
	     nuget.org and the fix is invisible to them. To ship an ILGPU/ fix you
	     MUST bump all four together:

	       (1) ILGPU/ILGPU.csproj                                  <Version>
	       (2) ILGPU.Algorithms/ILGPU.Algorithms.csproj            <Version>
	       (3) SpawnDev.ILGPU/SpawnDev.ILGPU.csproj                <Version>
	       (4) The TWO PackageReference lines IMMEDIATELY BELOW
	           (the !Exists branch) - in-repo dev uses ProjectReference and
	           does not see these; the published nuspec dependency does.

	     Then run _check-fork-version-sync.bat at repo root to verify all four
	     match before commit. CI runs the same check on every push. If you skip
	     step (4) the consumer ships rc.N with stale Fork 2.0.x and the fix
	     never reaches the IR. This is exactly what bit rc.28 on 2026-04-28.
	     ===================================================================== -->
	<!-- Conditional fork reference: in-repo dev uses ProjectReference (fast iteration); external consumers use PackageReference via NuGet -->
	<ItemGroup Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\ILGPU\ILGPU.csproj')">
		<ProjectReference Include="..\ILGPU\ILGPU.csproj" />
		<ProjectReference Include="..\ILGPU.Algorithms\ILGPU.Algorithms.csproj" />
	</ItemGroup>
	<ItemGroup Condition="!Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\ILGPU\ILGPU.csproj')">
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU.Fork" Version="2.3.3-local.3" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU.Algorithms.Fork" Version="2.3.3-local.3" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="Assets\icon-128.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\LICENSE.txt" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="wwwroot\icon-128.png" />
		<!-- precompiled-shaders Layer 2: the build automation .targets is auto-imported by NuGet
		     from build/. Gated OFF by default (<SpawnDevPrecompileShaders>), so consumers that do
		     not opt in are unaffected. -->
		<None Include="build\SpawnDev.ILGPU.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="build\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- precompiled-shaders Layer 2: bundle the precompiler worker (+ its dependency closure) into
	     the package tools/ folder so a consumer that opts in gets it with zero config. Gated on
	     PackPrecompilerTool (default FALSE) so the everyday build/pack stays fast - publishing the
	     tool is only done for an explicit release pack:
	         dotnet pack SpawnDev.ILGPU/SpawnDev.ILGPU.csproj -c Release -p:PackPrecompilerTool=true
	     When not bundled, the .targets emits a clear error pointing at <SpawnDevPrecompilerToolPath>. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(PackPrecompilerTool)' == 'true'">
		<TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>$(TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage);_BundlePrecompilerTool</TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>
	</PropertyGroup>
	<Target Name="_BundlePrecompilerTool">
		<Exec Command="dotnet publish &quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\SpawnDev.ILGPU.Precompiler\SpawnDev.ILGPU.Precompiler.csproj&quot; -c $(Configuration) -o &quot;$(IntermediateOutputPath)precompiler-tool&quot;" />
		<ItemGroup>
			<_PrecompilerToolFiles Include="$(IntermediateOutputPath)precompiler-tool\**\*.*" />
			<TfmSpecificPackageFile Include="@(_PrecompilerToolFiles)">
				<PackagePath>tools\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</PackagePath>
			</TfmSpecificPackageFile>
		</ItemGroup>
	</Target>

	<!-- =================================================================================================
	     THIS TARGET IS THE REASON _publish-nuget.bat USES `pack`. DO NOT "FIX" THAT TO `build`.
	     =================================================================================================
	     TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage is a PACK-time extension point. Under
	     GeneratePackageOnBuild the target above still RUNS - it publishes the tool into
	     obj\...\precompiler-tool and the DLLs are sitting right there - but its files never reach the
	     package. MEASURED 2026-09-01, same property, same configuration:

	         dotnet build -c Release -p:PackPrecompilerTool=true  ->  1,529,731 bytes,  0 tools\ entries
	         dotnet pack  -c Release -p:PackPrecompilerTool=true  ->  6,659,041 bytes, 15 tools\ entries

	     SpawnDev.ILGPU/_publish-nuget.bat is the ONLY publish bat of 247 in the workspace that packs, which
	     reads like an agent's mistake and is not. Converting it to `build` ships a package with no
	     precompiler worker, silently - both verbs exit 0 and both write a correctly-versioned nupkg.
	     Hooking the target to _GetPackageFiles instead was also tried, and also produced 0 tools\ entries.

	     ⚠️ AND THE PAYLOAD IS ONLY REGENERATED WHEN THE VERSION CHANGES. Packaging is incremental:
	         GenerateNuspec: Skipping target "GenerateNuspec" because all output files are up-to-date
	     Re-packing an already-packed version yields the 1.5 MB tool-less package, and deleting
	     bin\Release\*.nupkg AND obj\Release\*.nuspec does NOT change that - only an unseen version does.
	     That is why this works in practice (a release bumps the version) and why re-publishing the same
	     version would quietly not. The bat verifies the artifact and aborts on an empty tools\ rather than
	     trusting the exit code.

	     ⚠️ When it does run fully the glob OVER-collects: the Precompiler's publish drags in this project's
	     own WebGPU\, WebGL\ and Wasm\ asset folders (72 DLLs / 105 MB uncompressed -> a 47.7 MB nupkg).
	     6.6 MB is the intended shape, so narrow the glob before changing the invalidation behaviour.

	     PROPER FIX, for whoever has the time: SpawnDev.SpawnJS.WebWorkers bundles its rollup tooling with a
	     STATIC <Content Include="tasks\**" Pack="true" PackagePath="tasks"> over PRE-BUILT, COMMITTED
	     files. Static items exist at project evaluation, so GeneratePackageOnBuild packs them and no pack
	     step is needed at all. Adopting that here means deciding how the precompiler's output is produced
	     and whether it gets committed - a real design call, not a mechanical swap. See
	     _DevComms/2026-09-01-tuvok-TO-geordi-ilgpu-publish-bat-is-the-last-pack-based-one.md -->


	<ItemGroup>
		<Content Remove="wwwroot\icon-128.png" />
		<!-- Wasm/repro/ is tracked debugging-repro scratch (V8 barrier bug repros, .wasm/.wat/.json
		     fixtures). The Razor SDK auto-sweeps its *.json (manifest/stats/sitemap) into the package
		     as content/ + contentFiles/ - junk that has no business in the shipped library. Exclude
		     the whole repro tree from packing (it is not a build input). -->
		<Content Remove="Wasm\repro\**" />
		<None Remove="Wasm\repro\**" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="windows" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="linux" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="macos" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
